1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,122
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,122 --> 00:00:06,021
This is part one of the heart disease patient analysis
project.

3
00:00:06,021 --> 00:00:14,032
In this video we walk through the Python notebook, step by
step: what was done, why, and what we found.

4
00:00:15,432 --> 00:00:16,929
Here is the scenario.

5
00:00:16,929 --> 00:00:23,842
A hospital cardiology department has records for 303
patients who were checked for heart disease.

6
00:00:23,842 --> 00:00:29,472
Management wants to improve screening, triage, and the use
of diagnostic tests.

7
00:00:29,472 --> 00:00:38,452
The notebook answers ten business problems, listed on the
right, and its outputs feed a Power BI dashboard and a
presentation.

8
00:00:39,852 --> 00:00:41,690
This is the process flow.

9
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:45,217
First we set up the tools and load the raw file.

10
00:00:45,217 --> 00:00:49,407
Then we check the data, clean it, and create new columns.

11
00:00:49,407 --> 00:00:52,714
Next we analyse business problems two to ten.

12
00:00:52,714 --> 00:00:57,785
Finally we export four files for Power BI and summarise the
findings.

13
00:00:57,785 --> 00:01:02,563
The bar at the top of each scene shows where we are in this
flow.

14
00:01:03,963 --> 00:01:05,440
Step one is setup.

15
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:13,483
We import pandas for tables, Matplotlib and Seaborn for
charts, and SciPy for one statistics test.

16
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:15,946
Then we read the raw CSV file.

17
00:01:15,946 --> 00:01:23,004
It has 303 rows and 14 columns: thirteen clinical
measurements plus the target column.

18
00:01:23,004 --> 00:01:26,534
We look at it with head, info and describe.

19
00:01:27,934 --> 00:01:32,634
Step two answers business problem one: can we trust the
data?

20
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:36,024
We check for missing values: there are none.

21
00:01:36,024 --> 00:01:38,412
We find one duplicated patient.

22
00:01:38,412 --> 00:01:45,116
And six cells hold codes outside the valid range: ca equals
four, and thal equals zero.

23
00:01:45,116 --> 00:01:51,819
Later we also count twenty outliers, which we keep because
they are medically possible.

24
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:55,763
Next comes the most important finding.

25
00:01:55,763 --> 00:02:01,589
We counted every code in our file and compared it with the
original UCI Cleveland file.

26
00:02:01,589 --> 00:02:06,945
The counts match within one patient, which tells us what
each code really means.

27
00:02:06,945 --> 00:02:13,373
And it shows that target equals one means no heart disease,
the opposite of the data dictionary.

28
00:02:13,373 --> 00:02:20,203
Read the old way, women would have three times the disease
rate of men, which is clinically backwards.

29
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:22,278
So the label must be corrected.

30
00:02:23,678 --> 00:02:27,437
Step three cleans the data in five simple moves.

31
00:02:27,437 --> 00:02:30,648
Drop the duplicate, leaving 302 patients.

32
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:35,425
Turn the invalid codes into missing values, shown as
unknown.

33
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:40,751
Create the corrected outcome: heart disease equals one minus
target.

34
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:43,962
Replace every code with a readable label.

35
00:02:43,962 --> 00:02:47,094
And rename the columns to plain English.

36
00:02:48,494 --> 00:02:53,214
We also look for outliers with box plots and the I Q R rule.

37
00:02:53,214 --> 00:02:57,933
Twenty values are flagged, for example a cholesterol of 564.

38
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:04,226
All are medically possible, so we keep them and flag them
for the clinical team.

39
00:03:05,626 --> 00:03:07,774
Step four creates new columns.

40
00:03:07,774 --> 00:03:22,309
With pandas cut, we group raw numbers into meaningful bands:
ten-year age groups, American Heart Association blood
pressure stages, cholesterol bands, and bands for maximum
heart rate and S T depression.

41
00:03:22,309 --> 00:03:24,887
Bands make every chart easy to read.

42
00:03:26,287 --> 00:03:27,836
Now the analysis.

43
00:03:27,836 --> 00:03:31,934
Business problem two: how big is the problem?

44
00:03:31,934 --> 00:03:35,486
138 of 302 patients have heart disease.

45
00:03:35,486 --> 00:03:38,947
That is 46 percent, almost one in two.

46
00:03:38,947 --> 00:03:43,319
The typical patient is a man in his mid fifties.

47
00:03:43,319 --> 00:03:48,328
We use 46 percent as the baseline for every comparison.

48
00:03:49,728 --> 00:03:51,217
Problem three: age.

49
00:03:51,217 --> 00:03:58,192
The disease rate climbs from 27 percent under forty to 60
percent at sixty to sixty nine.

50
00:03:58,192 --> 00:04:02,346
Patients with disease are six years older on average.

51
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:06,500
Recommendation: start routine screening at age fifty.

52
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:09,373
Problem four: sex.

53
00:04:09,373 --> 00:04:19,111
Men are more than twice as likely to have heart disease, 55
percent against 25 percent, and the gap holds at every age.

54
00:04:19,111 --> 00:04:25,167
Recommendation: screen men from forty to forty five, and
women from fifty.

55
00:04:26,567 --> 00:04:29,377
Problem five is the most surprising.

56
00:04:29,377 --> 00:04:34,764
Patients with no chest pain have the highest rate of all, 73
percent.

57
00:04:34,764 --> 00:04:38,043
Classic typical angina is only 30 percent.

58
00:04:38,043 --> 00:04:42,727
So a rule of chest pain equals urgent would miss most cases.

59
00:04:42,727 --> 00:04:46,162
Silent presentations should be fast-tracked.

60
00:04:47,562 --> 00:04:49,504
Problem six: routine checks.

61
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:58,657
Blood pressure, cholesterol and fasting blood sugar barely
separate patients, and the differences are not statistically
significant.

62
00:04:58,657 --> 00:05:03,719
Keep them for prevention, but don't use them to decide who
gets referred.

63
00:05:05,119 --> 00:05:08,332
Problem seven: the exercise stress test.

64
00:05:08,332 --> 00:05:11,464
All four measures show strong patterns.

65
00:05:11,464 --> 00:05:19,014
Severe S T depression means 86 percent disease, and a low
maximum heart rate means 79 percent.

66
00:05:19,014 --> 00:05:24,235
The stress test is the best value mid-cost test, so use it
early.

67
00:05:25,635 --> 00:05:27,446
Problem eight: imaging.

68
00:05:27,446 --> 00:05:36,661
Each extra narrowed vessel raises the rate, from 26 to 85
percent, and a reversible thallium defect means 76 percent.

69
00:05:36,661 --> 00:05:43,906
Imaging is expensive, so use it for patients already flagged
by symptoms or the stress test.

70
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:48,806
Problem nine ranks every factor on one scale.

71
00:05:48,806 --> 00:05:55,340
A chi-square test gives Cramer's V, from zero for no link to
one for a perfect link.

72
00:05:55,340 --> 00:06:01,952
Thallium scan, chest-pain type and narrowed vessels come
first, then the stress test.

73
00:06:01,952 --> 00:06:03,896
Routine checks come last.

74
00:06:03,896 --> 00:06:09,652
So the budget order is: symptoms, then stress test, then
targeted imaging.

75
00:06:11,052 --> 00:06:13,387
Problem ten builds risk tiers.

76
00:06:13,387 --> 00:06:17,903
Each patient gets one point for each of six warning signs.

77
00:06:17,903 --> 00:06:22,885
Low is zero or one sign, medium two or three, high four or
more.

78
00:06:22,885 --> 00:06:30,903
The high tier has 93 percent disease, and holds 62 percent
of all cases in just 30 percent of patients.

79
00:06:30,903 --> 00:06:34,562
That is a powerful way to prioritise follow-up.

80
00:06:35,962 --> 00:06:40,520
Step six exports four CSV files to the processed data
folder.

81
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:47,320
The clean patient table, the factor ranking, the data
quality log, and the data dictionary.

82
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:53,298
We also add sort-order columns, so Power BI shows the groups
in a logical order.

83
00:06:53,298 --> 00:06:57,333
These four files are exactly what the dashboard reads.

84
00:06:58,733 --> 00:07:01,595
To summarise, five recommendations.

85
00:07:01,595 --> 00:07:03,148
Fix the data first.

86
00:07:03,148 --> 00:07:06,336
Screen from age fifty, and men earlier.

87
00:07:06,336 --> 00:07:09,525
Fast-track patients with no chest pain.

88
00:07:09,525 --> 00:07:15,248
Test in the right order: symptoms, stress test, then
targeted imaging.

89
00:07:15,248 --> 00:07:18,355
And prioritise follow-up by risk tier.

90
00:07:18,355 --> 00:07:28,983
Remember the limitations: a small sample from one hospital,
links rather than causes, and tiers that need testing on new
patients.

91
00:07:30,383 --> 00:07:32,474
That completes the notebook.

92
00:07:32,474 --> 00:07:38,973
In part two, we will see how these four files become an
interactive Power BI dashboard.

93
00:07:38,973 --> 00:07:40,691
Thank you for watching.
